Second ionization removes one electron from each singly charged ion with p configurations 2p^1 for \text{C}^{+} and 2p^2 for \text{N}^{+} and 2p^3 for \text{O}^{+} and 2p^4 for \text{F}^{+}
The half-filled 2p^3 shell makes removal from \text{O}^{+} hardest while fewer p electrons make removal easier
Order is C < N < F < O which matches option A → (A)

24789:
Ligand field strength follows the spectrochemical series \text{CN}^{-} > \text{en} > \text{NH}_3 > \text{H}_2\text{O} > \text{Cl}^{-}
Splitting follows the same order with \Delta_o largest for \text{CN}^{-} and smallest for \text{Cl}^{-}
Absorption wavelength satisfies \lambda \propto \frac{1}{\Delta_o} so the wavelength order reverses the field order
This gives the sequence of option A → (A)

24796:
Carbon comes from \text{CO}_2 so m_C = \frac{12}{44} \times 0.1837 = 0.0501 \text{ g}
Hydrogen comes from \text{H}_2\text{O} so m_H = \frac{2}{18} \times 0.15 = 0.0167 \text{ g}
Oxygen follows by difference with m_O = 0.25 - 0.0501 - 0.0167 = 0.1832 \text{ g}
Hence \% O = \frac{0.1832}{0.25} \times 100 = 73.3 \approx 73 → (73)

24802:
Complete the square in f(t) = -t^2 + 3t - 2 = -(t - \frac{3}{2})^2 + \frac{1}{4}
Hence the maximum value is \frac{1}{4}, so e = \frac{1}{4}
For \frac{x^2}{a^2} + \frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1 with a > b, the latus rectum is \frac{2b^2}{a} = 30 and e^2 = 1 - \frac{b^2}{a^2}
From e^2 = \frac{1}{16}, \frac{b^2}{a^2} = 1 - \frac{1}{16} = \frac{15}{16}, so b^2 = \frac{15a^2}{16}
Then \frac{2b^2}{a} = \frac{15a}{8} = 30 gives a = 16
So a^2 = 256 and b^2 = 240, giving a^2 + b^2 = 256 + 240 = 496
The value 496 is option B → (B)

24818:
Put a = \frac{1}{3} and b = \frac{4}{7}, so group 1 is a + b, group 2 is a^2 + ab + b^2, and so on
The whole series is every a^m b^n with m + n \geq 1, each pair occurring exactly once
Hence S = (\sum_{m \geq 0} a^m)(\sum_{n \geq 0} b^n) - 1 = \frac{1}{1 - a} \times \frac{1}{1 - b} - 1
Here \frac{1}{1 - a} = \frac{1}{1 - \frac{1}{3}} = \frac{3}{2} and \frac{1}{1 - b} = \frac{1}{1 - \frac{4}{7}} = \frac{7}{3}
So S = \frac{3}{2} \times \frac{7}{3} - 1 = \frac{7}{2} - 1 = \frac{5}{2}
The value \frac{5}{2} is option D → (D)

24805:
Combine the cross terms as (\vec{a} + 2\vec{b}) \times \vec{c} = \vec{0}, so \vec{c} is parallel to \vec{a} + 2\vec{b}
Adding gives \vec{a} + 2\vec{b} = (2 - 2)\hat{i} + (-5 + 2)\hat{j} + (5 + 6)\hat{k} = -3\hat{j} + 11\hat{k}
Hence write \vec{c} = \lambda(-3\hat{j} + 11\hat{k})
Also \vec{a} - \vec{b} = 3\hat{i} - 6\hat{j} + 2\hat{k}, and dotting with \vec{c} gives (\vec{a} - \vec{b}) \cdot \vec{c} = \lambda(18 + 22) = 40\lambda = -97
Hence \lambda = -\frac{97}{40}
Since \vec{c} = -3\lambda\hat{j} + 11\lambda\hat{k}, crossing with \hat{k} gives \vec{c} \times \hat{k} = -3\lambda\hat{i}, so |\vec{c} \times \hat{k}|^2 = 9\lambda^2
Evaluating gives 9\lambda^2 = \frac{9 \times 9409}{1600} = \frac{84681}{1600} \approx 52.93
Since 52.93 matches none of the listed options 193, 233, 205, 218, no listed option follows and the question carries full marks for all attempted → (Bonus)

24812:
The direction vectors are \vec{d_1} = 2\hat{i} + (1 - \alpha)\hat{j} - \alpha\hat{k} and \vec{d_2} = \alpha\hat{i} + \alpha\hat{j} + 2\hat{k}
Their cross product is \vec{d_1} \times \vec{d_2} = (\alpha^2 - 2\alpha + 2)\hat{i} - (\alpha^2 + 4)\hat{j} + (\alpha^2 + \alpha)\hat{k}
Joining (-1, 4, 1) to (1, 1, 2) gives (2, -3, 1), whose dot product with the cross product is 6\alpha^2 - 3\alpha + 16
Hence the shortest distance squared is \frac{(6\alpha^2 - 3\alpha + 16)^2}{(\alpha^2 - 2\alpha + 2)^2 + (\alpha^2 + 4)^2 + (\alpha^2 + \alpha)^2}
Setting this equal to 2 clears denominators to 30\alpha^4 - 32\alpha^3 + 167\alpha^2 - 80\alpha + 216 = 0
This quartic stays above 206, so it has no real root, and indeed the shortest distance never falls below about 3.32, well above \sqrt{2}
Since no value of \alpha gives distance \sqrt{2}, there is nothing to sum and no listed option follows, so the question carries full marks for all attempted → (Bonus)
